WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:01.510
(air whooshing)
(clock ticking)

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:03.480
- I would argue that I'm
still not great at it,

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.160
and I was borderline atrocious at it

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.323
the first 15 to 16 years of my career.

00:00:08.323 --> 00:00:10.520
(high-pitched music)
(men exclaiming)

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.852
You got your perspective.

00:00:11.852 --> 00:00:13.510
(upbeat music)

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:15.735
(crowd roaring)

00:00:15.735 --> 00:00:17.406
I just wanna be happy,
don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:17.406 --> 00:00:18.310
(camera snapping)

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.809
(funky keyboard music)

00:00:20.809 --> 00:00:22.440
Hey everybody, it's Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.160
I was, I've already decided,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.540
I don't think my team even knows this,

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.200
that I'm changing the
name of "Interrupted By,"

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.200
yeah, because, just like
reading the comments,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:31.833
I just don't, I think it triples-down on

00:00:31.833 --> 00:00:33.270
something I don't want it to,

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.460
so the reality is that
show's now canceled.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.960
And so now I just interview friends,

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:40.290
people I'm interested in,

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.430
and I have a really dear friend here

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.440
who I haven't seen in a little while.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:43.836
So it's nice to see you.

00:00:43.836 --> 00:00:46.610
Who, we're gonna talk about music and tech

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:48.290
and things of that nature.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.542
Like I've said, some of the people I know

00:00:50.542 --> 00:00:52.217
occasionally reach out and they're like,

00:00:52.217 --> 00:00:55.120
"Hey, I wanna yap and talk about stuff."

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.790
And I'm like yeah, let's do it.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.509
So, no prep, I literally just walked in

00:00:58.509 --> 00:01:00.060
and here we are.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.850
So, why don't you tell the Vayner Nation,

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:03.683
all the people listening,

00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:06.030
who you are, what you do,
and then we'll get into it.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:06.863
- Sure.

00:01:06.863 --> 00:01:09.472
I'm Milana, I'm the
founder and CEO of a music,

00:01:09.472 --> 00:01:11.360
can you hear me, is this better?

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:12.647
- Yeah, probably better.
- Sorry.

00:01:12.647 --> 00:01:14.007
(muffled speaking)
Oh, got it, okay.

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:15.950
I'm so new to this whole podcast thing.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:16.783
So you're gonna have
to teach me the ropes.

00:01:16.783 --> 00:01:18.080
- Have you ever done a podcast, Milana?

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:19.300
- I've done a couple.
- [Gary] Okay, good.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.340
- But this is a new mic
setup, I haven't seen that.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.150
- Yeah, it's, we're
very crazy around here.

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.280
- No, you guys are very like,
you got it all together,

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.826
it's all set up, I love it.

00:01:26.826 --> 00:01:30.330
So, again, I'm Milana,
I'm the co-founder and CEO

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.210
of a company called Stem.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.850
And our mission is to help artists

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:36.120
build sustainable businesses.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.270
And when we talk about artists,

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.246
it's primarily musicians today.

00:01:39.246 --> 00:01:44.246
And the platform allows a
lot of independent artists

00:01:44.417 --> 00:01:47.660
to get their music on Apple and Spotify,

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.210
all the major platforms.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:52.670
And the thing that we
do that no one else does

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:56.150
is we automate the splits
on all of the payouts

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:57.100
to all the collaborators.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.089
So if you see a collaboration
with a bunch of rappers,

00:02:00.089 --> 00:02:02.380
and it's out independently,

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:04.060
chances are it's through Stem.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:04.950
- Understood.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.421
So, up for full disclosure,

00:02:06.421 --> 00:02:08.580
I'm an investor in this company,

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:09.960
we've known each other a long time.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:10.793
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:02:10.793 --> 00:02:12.120
- Phil is in the building,
which is always nice,

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.600
good to see you, Phil.
- [Phil] Nice to see you.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.200
- Back there, who's been staying
close to this investment.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.660
And so how's it been going?

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.180
And when you talk about distributions,

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.870
the thing that most drew
me to the opportunity,

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.670
what is it, like, four years ago now?

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:28.530
- It'll be four years ago
since I told you about it,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.370
five years ago since
we started the company.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:31.203
- [Gary] Jesus, so--

00:02:31.203 --> 00:02:32.036
- Or, no, that's a lie,

00:02:32.036 --> 00:02:33.474
five years since I told you
about it, four years since

00:02:33.474 --> 00:02:34.501
we started it.
- [Gary] Started it,

00:02:34.501 --> 00:02:35.334
makes sense.

00:02:35.334 --> 00:02:37.260
So, one of the things I really liked was,

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.435
the YouTube ecosystem was
super-confusing at the time.

00:02:39.435 --> 00:02:41.440
- [Milana] Yep.
- It still may be.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:42.940
That's really my question.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.050
Has, you know it's one
thing, to your point,

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:47.460
when three people, two
signed, one unsigned,

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.780
collab on a song and
it's super-complicated,

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:51.030
I've learned that lesson.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:54.700
I had a song that I made
for Clouds and Dirt,

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:58.710
my sneaker, with Gunna,
Tierra Whack, and Tee Grizzly

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.740
that was 38 seconds from
being uploaded to Spotify,

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.070
launching with my sneaker,

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:05.823
that died because it just died.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.660
How's that been working

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.080
and how has the YouTube
Ecosystem worked out

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:12.690
and what has emerged, whether it's TikTok

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.070
or other things of that nature?

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.270
- Yeah, definitely, so
YouTube is still, I believe,

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:18.610
the largest music platform,
for streaming, right?

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:19.690
And I think a big part of it

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:21.460
is because it's totally global.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.178
Spotify didn't launch globally.

00:03:23.178 --> 00:03:24.960
Initially, at least,

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.610
and now it's continuing to grow that way.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:27.680
- [Gary] Yep.
- But when we started,

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.590
the other problem I wanted to solve

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:32.150
was that there were all these
content creators on YouTube

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:34.000
that were collaborating, right?

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.400
And when they would want to post a video,

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.570
and it was two different channels,

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:39.180
they would post the same video twice

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.260
and bifurcate the views.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:41.345
And the reason they would do that

00:03:41.345 --> 00:03:43.080
is because they wanted
to each monetize it.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.127
And so we thought to ourselves,

00:03:44.127 --> 00:03:46.010
"Well, why don't we just
split the revenue from it?"

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:46.843
Right?

00:03:46.843 --> 00:03:48.860
When you have apps like
Venmo and all these others

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.130
that make it so easy to
send money on the internet.

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.490
If you're making money on the internet,

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:53.410
why can't you just share it?

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:54.310
- So what happens now?

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:56.210
Like if me and Phil both have channels,

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.790
like does one pick to air it on theirs

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:00.350
and just kicks it to the other person?

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:02.010
- Yeah, I mean they would
Venmo each other the money,

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:03.900
unless you go through Stem,
you register the video,

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.810
well, you can post it on both channels,

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:07.210
it can live on hundreds of channels.

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:10.000
We recognize when the
video's being consumed,

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.170
we collect the revenue
from YouTube directly,

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:14.170
and then each of you guys
have your own account

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.190
and you get paid every month.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:18.940
- So, what's the biggest
learning you've had

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:20.940
in the past four years of going from

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.760
being an executive in companies and,

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:23.860
actually, give a little context.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.180
What was your career
prior to co-founding Stem?

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.760
- Yeah, so I was one of the first

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.150
digital media talent agents in Hollywood.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:33.240
I was brought on as an
agent at the age of 21,

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.009
which is ridiculous that
they would even hire me.

00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:36.360
(laughs)

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.940
But my job was discovering
talent on the internet

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.190
and helping them break their concepts

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.010
into traditional media so,

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:45.810
Issa Rae I signed when she
had "Awkward Black Girl"

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.950
the channel on YouTube, got her book deal,

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:49.320
and then we packaged the TV show

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:51.910
and sold it to HBO, which
is now called "Insecure."

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:53.860
So it was being a part
of a lot of the talent

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:55.220
that was coming off the internet--

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:56.402
- Who was the first person you signed,

00:04:56.402 --> 00:04:57.880
do you remember?

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.953
- That's a great question.

00:04:59.953 --> 00:05:02.982
- Was it Toronto Tar-tar?
(laughing)

00:05:02.982 --> 00:05:03.815
Phil?

00:05:03.815 --> 00:05:04.648
- I wish.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:07.580
I don't know, but I've
had really fun clients,

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:09.520
people like Josh Strovsky
who you guys know

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:11.556
as the Fat Jew.

00:05:11.556 --> 00:05:13.620
I actually think one of
the first things I signed--

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.051
- Did you sign him first
or was he already signed

00:05:16.051 --> 00:05:17.692
and then you kinda got him?

00:05:17.692 --> 00:05:19.540
- (laughing) No, we signed him because

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.719
we signed a Twitter feed
called "White Girl Problems."

00:05:22.719 --> 00:05:23.552
- Oh, I remember.

00:05:23.552 --> 00:05:25.762
- That was one of the
early clients, I think.

00:05:25.762 --> 00:05:27.760
- And he was behind that?

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.660
- He was close with the
writers of that, yeah.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:30.493
- [Gary] Got it.

00:05:30.493 --> 00:05:32.400
- And so we got connected
to him, all through that

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:33.233
kind of stuff.
- [Gary] Through that crew.

00:05:33.233 --> 00:05:34.330
- Yep.
- That was their crew.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:36.250
- It was fun, but that's
the thing that I noticed

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:37.990
is that more and more independent creators

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.340
were making real money online

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:40.960
and building real businesses.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.860
And to me, it started to
really dispel this notion

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.570
of a starving artist, right?

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:49.260
Like, you and I have similar roots,

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.410
we're both from Babruysk
area and our families,

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.190
Russian immigrants to the states.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:55.980
And when I told my parents in high school

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:58.400
that I wanted to go to film
school and be a producer,

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.341
they're like, "That's a great
hobby, but not a career."

00:06:00.341 --> 00:06:01.174
- [Gary] Right.

00:06:01.174 --> 00:06:02.850
- And that crushed me.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.186
And so what I wanted to
do was to help artists

00:06:06.186 --> 00:06:08.640
be able to not only build careers,

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.500
but businesses off of doing
what they're great at.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:14.970
And I felt like the thing that
was really preventing them

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.730
was just tools, and more importantly,

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.730
visibility into their finances.

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:21.250
So the thing that I've learned

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.920
is that it's amazing how

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:28.510
how easy it is for a person to understand

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:31.080
their business when
they see their numbers.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.615
And numbers with a dollar
sign in front of them.

00:06:32.615 --> 00:06:33.448
- [Gary] Yep.

00:06:33.448 --> 00:06:36.717
- And like artists have
always been told, "Hey--

00:06:36.717 --> 00:06:37.887
- "I've got this covered."

00:06:37.887 --> 00:06:38.737
- "I've got this covered,

00:06:38.737 --> 00:06:39.570
"let someone else focus on the business."

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.030
- "You worry about being
creative and enjoy."

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:42.440
- Totally, and I think that's bullshit.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:43.273
- Yep.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:48.036
What was the biggest surprise to you

00:06:48.036 --> 00:06:52.040
versus the ideology of what you
thought entrepreneurship was

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:53.140
four years ago--
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:55.712
- To now it's true realities
now that you live in it?

00:06:55.712 --> 00:06:59.720
- I think it's all about the
people around you, right?

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:02.150
How do you really

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.570
align people with your vision?

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:08.730
And it takes so much repetition

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:11.580
to get people I think to truly understand

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:13.970
what they're rowing towards as a team.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:16.200
And I think that you, at least
when I would read about it

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.470
in books or hear other
people talking about it,

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:20.620
it sounded like "Oh,
it can't be that hard."

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:22.670
But it's actually, I think,
the fundamental thing

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.150
that you have to get right.

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:24.983
- Yeah, I understand.

00:07:24.983 --> 00:07:26.550
I mean it's really funny,

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.250
I had an employee that's worked
at Vayner for seven years

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:32.360
come into my office last
year, late last year,

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:35.690
and say, "Hey, like," super-frustrated,

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:37.237
and the person was like,

00:07:37.237 --> 00:07:39.340
"What are we even doing here?"

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:40.890
And I started laughing

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:42.590
because we had a great, warm relationship

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:43.880
and I didn't feel like
it was disrespectful.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:48.020
And I'm like, "I have only
repeated 700,000 times

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:50.520
to your face that we're
building a platform to buy

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.540
brands when the economy
collapses" to like,

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:54.950
it was like super-laughable.
- [Milana] Right.

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:57.150
- But I think it's a
really good demonstration

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:58.770
of what you're talking about.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.530
Because

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:01.930
people,

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:02.990
when they're employees,

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:04.830
and you've been one so you know this,

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.220
inherently subconsciously have to have

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:09.480
some level of fear base in them,

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.870
that the company may be
not telling you the truth.

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:14.230
It's just the inherent human nature of

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:17.444
the employer/employee relationship and so

00:08:17.444 --> 00:08:21.760
reaffirming the mission or what
you're trying to accomplish,

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:25.000
even if it's super-simplistic,
- [Milana] Oh yeah.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.180
- Is oftentimes not

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:29.900
not consumed because

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:32.820
one has to worry about
themselves as an employee.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:35.570
And is always worried that
the other shoe might drop.

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:38.100
- I mean, it's crazy, even
when you're trying to be

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:40.440
as clear as possible, right?

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.600
Clarity is one of our values

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.480
because we're dealing in a business

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.110
that's considered really
intentionally opaque

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:48.720
and we're trying to change all that.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.440
But like we out of every
all hands show the business,

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:52.294
the company metrics--

00:08:52.294 --> 00:08:53.127
- How many employees are there now?

00:08:53.127 --> 00:08:53.960
- We're about 35.

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:55.010
- [Gary] That's awesome.

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:55.843
- It's a fun time.

00:08:55.843 --> 00:08:57.130
- What about firing somebody?

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:58.060
- That's fucking hard.

00:08:58.060 --> 00:08:59.000
- Yeah.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:08:59.870
Was that hard for you?

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:02.420
- It was initially because I
didn't know how to do it well.

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:03.253
- [Gary] Right.

00:09:03.253 --> 00:09:06.830
- But I feel like if you're
good at managing expectations

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:09.500
and setting really clear goals,

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.740
then by the time you have
to have that conversation

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:15.020
with someone that's about
them parting ways with you,

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:15.860
like they should already know,

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:16.693
it shouldn't be a surprise

00:09:16.693 --> 00:09:17.890
and it shouldn't be a hard conversation.

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:20.200
- Yeah, I would argue that
I'm still not great at it

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:22.880
and I was borderline atrocious at it

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.286
the first 15 to 16 years of my career.

00:09:25.286 --> 00:09:26.720
I just hated it.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:28.138
- How'd you get better?

00:09:28.138 --> 00:09:31.010
- (sighing deeply) AJ helped,
when AJ came to Vayner,

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:32.170
when I started with AJ,

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:35.350
he was much better at
it than me with candor,

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:37.360
which I think is the main part

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:38.910
to what you were talking about.

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:42.510
Just wanting to be better.
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:44.660
- You know, just realizing this was

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:48.601
one of the flaws in my system
that I would shock people.

00:09:48.601 --> 00:09:51.230
It's nice to make it lovely and bubbly

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:53.750
and awesome 24/7, 365.

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:56.930
But if the movie ends with you firing them

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:58.910
and they're flabbergasted
'cause you've given them

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:02.260
no negative feedback, that's not good.

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.983
And I just had to get to
that place where I was like,

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:07.900
just had to get to that
place where I realized

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:10.042
it was one of my weaknesses.

00:10:10.042 --> 00:10:12.293
- Have you ever tried to get
someone back that you fired?

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:14.530
- No.
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:16.330
- Because I desperately don't wanna fire.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:17.163
Like,
(Milana laughing)

00:10:17.163 --> 00:10:21.800
I really, like, it's awfully
hard to get fired in Gary Land,

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:24.680
you really are in DNF land around either

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:26.820
your skillset or your personality

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:28.590
and how you affect everybody else.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:31.920
So, no, I've never tried
to get anybody who,

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:33.450
in the history of my
life I've never tried to

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:35.750
rehire somebody I fired.
(Milana chuckling)

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:37.610
I've tried to get people
back that have left.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:38.900
- [Milana] Right.

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:41.193
- But even that's been remarkably rare.

00:10:42.926 --> 00:10:46.030
But, yeah, that was a
real struggle for me.

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:46.863
What about

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:48.560
what about

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:56.220
somebody who's great at their job but is

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.070
not nice to the other boys and girls?

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:00.390
How much of a challenge has that been

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:02.690
with making a decision based on

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:05.640
culture versus output of skill?

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:06.473
- Yeah.

00:11:06.473 --> 00:11:07.510
- [Gary] And where do you
sit on that currently?

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:08.883
- I have no tolerance for that.

00:11:08.883 --> 00:11:09.716
- [Gary] Okay.

00:11:09.716 --> 00:11:10.900
- So, we had someone who was

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:13.940
an incredibly talented
person in their job,

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:16.480
but was emotionally abusive.
- [Gary] Yep.

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:20.290
- And had to part ways with them because--

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:21.810
- How long did you sit on it?

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:23.150
And how long did you work on it?

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:23.983
- So, that's a great question.

00:11:23.983 --> 00:11:27.770
The person was with the
company for just about a year.

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:29.790
- When did you become
aware of, like, "Uh-oh"?

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:31.630
- Well, when they started reporting to me.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:32.463
- [Gary] Interesting.

00:11:32.463 --> 00:11:34.250
- Because they had previous bosses

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:37.920
they were two layers below and
those people sheltered that.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:39.980
And I had heard inklings.
- [Gary] Yep.

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:41.920
- But I hadn't experienced it firsthand.

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:42.753
- [Gary] Yep.

00:11:42.753 --> 00:11:45.750
- And when I did, I was like,
I have no tolerance for this.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.420
- And so did you try to reform

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:49.250
or was it because you had inklings,

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:50.530
you kinda knew it was in the air

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:51.670
and you were like, "Fuck it",

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:53.169
this is the affirmation I needed?

00:11:53.169 --> 00:11:54.017
- This was the affirmation,

00:11:54.017 --> 00:11:54.850
I didn't really need to sit on it.

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:56.720
I think I moved within 72 hours.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:58.530
- What about the industry itself?

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.600
Like, how's the competitive landscape?

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:02.280
How is that helping artists, you know?

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:03.250
- Yeah.

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:04.530
I think it's a really exciting time.

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:06.440
There's so many different partners

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:08.330
for artists to get their
music out there, right?

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:09.540
We know about the major labels

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:12.230
but there's all these
self-serve platforms, companies,

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:13.728
like DistroKid and TuneCore.

00:12:13.728 --> 00:12:14.978
- [Gary] I love Pud.

00:12:14.978 --> 00:12:15.811
- Pud's awesome.

00:12:15.811 --> 00:12:16.644
- [Gary] He's the best.

00:12:16.644 --> 00:12:18.199
- I think he's a great human,
he's someone who I talk to--

00:12:18.199 --> 00:12:19.420
- Do you guys cross over a lot?

00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:20.520
- [Milana] We do.
- Are you competitive?

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:21.353
- [Milana] You know what, it's funny,

00:12:21.353 --> 00:12:23.230
- I always wondered that.

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:27.920
- People assume we're really
competitive with each other,

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:28.753
and, I don't know--

00:12:28.753 --> 00:12:30.500
- Because the products are competitive?

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:33.360
- Not really, like, they do
want them to seem similar.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:35.270
- The cliche overlap?
- [Milana] Totally.

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:36.470
- What do you think, you would overlap

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:37.303
of 50% of the things?

00:12:37.303 --> 00:12:39.460
30% 80?
- I think less and less so.

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:43.500
We are not, like his user base is a lot of

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:45.530
both hobbyists and artists.
- [Gary] Yep.

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:47.130
- Who are up and coming.
- [Gary] Yep.

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:50.230
- Our focus is really on artists who

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:52.900
either have established
management teams around them

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:54.720
or are further in their career cycles.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:55.553
- [Gary] I see.

00:12:55.553 --> 00:12:56.560
- So there might be a
little bit of overlap

00:12:56.560 --> 00:12:57.661
in the early days.

00:12:57.661 --> 00:12:59.269
- Sure, and then there's
preferences, right?

00:12:59.269 --> 00:13:00.311
- [Milana] And there's preferences.

00:13:00.311 --> 00:13:01.144
- Some people just like
one app over the other.

00:13:01.144 --> 00:13:02.660
- Exactly, if you wanna pay a flat fee

00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:05.130
or a subscription and you
don't really need much support,

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:07.167
with an account manager,
he's an amazing service.

00:13:07.167 --> 00:13:08.000
- [Gary] Yep.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:10.040
- If you want a bit more
hands-on strategy support,

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:12.840
and you want to have
an ecosystem around you

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:14.470
and you're collaborating with established

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:16.690
songwriters and producers
who need to get paid

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:18.880
and you need help navigating
publishers, we do that.

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:21.830
- Are you being used
by managers and others?

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:23.598
Are you a B-to-B function
to a lot of the--

00:13:23.598 --> 00:13:24.431
- [Milana] We're mostly a B-to-B function.

00:13:24.431 --> 00:13:25.264
- Makes sense.

00:13:25.264 --> 00:13:27.650
- Yep, so we are embedded within

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:29.030
a lot of the big management companies,

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:32.690
like Red Light, Maverick,
Rock Nation, Full Stop, etc.

00:13:32.690 --> 00:13:34.800
And I think that's where
we came into the market

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.620
was when I realized
there was an open spot.

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:38.100
There were all these
services that were serving

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:40.990
the longtail or DIY or the major labels

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:43.940
had their offerings for clients
who wanted a major label.

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:45.390
But if you were an up-and-coming artist

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:47.820
that had a manager that
represented The Migos

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:49.980
or Future or someone like that,

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:50.930
and you were up and coming

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:53.010
and you weren't ready for a major label,

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:54.590
what was the best service for them to use?

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:56.200
'Cause they needed to
get someone on the phone,

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:57.033
- [Gary] Yep.

00:13:57.033 --> 00:13:58.310
- They wanted to have a partner
they can collaborate with

00:13:58.310 --> 00:14:01.080
but they didn't wanna give up 30%

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:02.440
and so we came up with an offering

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:03.820
that we thought was really hitting

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:05.910
a sweet spot that was open in the market.

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:07.088
- Interesting.

00:14:07.088 --> 00:14:09.570
Let's take it up even a higher level.

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:10.403
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:14:10.403 --> 00:14:11.630
- What do you think is
interesting about music

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:13.470
and creators right now in general?

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:14.540
State of the union,
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:16.627
- To bring the most value
to everybody listening?

00:14:16.627 --> 00:14:18.677
- I think that the form of music

00:14:18.677 --> 00:14:21.690
and the purpose of music has so much more

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:24.290
than it ever has, right?

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:25.716
Let's talk about TikTok for a second.

00:14:25.716 --> 00:14:26.549
- [Gary] Okay.

00:14:26.549 --> 00:14:27.560
- So the purpose of music back in the day

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:30.930
was about education,
storytelling, saying something

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:32.540
and expressing yourself that other people

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:34.780
can't put into words which
is why you listen to songs,

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:36.480
you have that emotional attachment

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:39.160
because someone's saying
something that you can't feel,

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:40.080
- [Gary] Yep.
- Or you can't express.

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:41.860
I mean you feel it but
you can't express it.

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:44.170
With things like TikTok
and YouTube, right?

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:46.960
Music has taken a shape in which people

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:47.950
can make it their own

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:50.150
and make it the soundtrack of their lives.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:53.003
And so video on YouTube
has been a place for music

00:14:53.003 --> 00:14:56.220
to break through because
people were able to share

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.070
music video content of their own type,

00:14:58.070 --> 00:14:59.580
not high production quality.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:01.730
Like, things went viral because

00:15:01.730 --> 00:15:03.020
of the dances people were doing,

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:04.687
whether it was Silento's,

00:15:04.687 --> 00:15:07.800
I'm blanking on it, what was it called?

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:08.633
The song that he did?

00:15:08.633 --> 00:15:10.300
- I'm not sure.

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:11.823
- The dance, oh my God.

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:14.043
- Don't worry.

00:15:14.043 --> 00:15:15.660
Good news, everybody's
gonna leave comments

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:16.600
about what it is, keep going.

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:18.080
- [Milana] There we go.
- I'm sure you'll remember.

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:19.359
- It'll hit me in a second.

00:15:19.359 --> 00:15:20.820
- I know about the broom challenge.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:22.354
- Well that's that, right?

00:15:22.354 --> 00:15:23.849
- [Gary] (laughing) The
less I care about today.

00:15:23.849 --> 00:15:24.682
- Right.
- [Gary] Every day.

00:15:24.682 --> 00:15:25.515
- There's all these songs that went viral

00:15:25.515 --> 00:15:27.150
because people were
recording their own videos,

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:28.821
home videos, and posting it
with music in the background.

00:15:28.821 --> 00:15:29.654
- [Gary] Of course.

00:15:29.654 --> 00:15:31.510
- And with TikTok, it's like
the shorter version of that.

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:34.510
So we're seeing things blow up on TikTok

00:15:34.510 --> 00:15:36.450
because people are able to use

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:40.460
the song's 15-second clip as like the loop

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:41.630
and make a video to it.

00:15:41.630 --> 00:15:44.540
And so it's an exciting time because now,

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:45.737
if you're a musician you're thinking about

00:15:45.737 --> 00:15:47.737
"How do I want people to consume my music

00:15:47.737 --> 00:15:49.810
"and what's the best place
for me to launch it on?"

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:51.310
- Yeah, I understand.

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:52.609
- [Milana] So I think that's exciting.

00:15:52.609 --> 00:15:54.280
- And so what about

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:55.960
creators

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:57.660
because we focused on music a little bit

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:00.737
but I'm interested in your take on creator

00:16:00.737 --> 00:16:03.015
revenue splits or like how,

00:16:03.015 --> 00:16:06.620
how strategic are the
top 5000 creators getting

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:09.158
with collabs and things of that nature?

00:16:09.158 --> 00:16:11.040
- I mean, that's the best way to grow.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:12.208
- [Gary] Yep.
- When you collab, right?

00:16:12.208 --> 00:16:13.269
It's the way that you grow your audience.

00:16:13.269 --> 00:16:14.102
- [Gary] And share.

00:16:14.102 --> 00:16:15.500
- Into a different--

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:16.909
- [Gary] Audiences with each other.

00:16:16.909 --> 00:16:17.742
- Exactly.
- [Gary] Yep.

00:16:17.742 --> 00:16:19.720
- So I think it's
exciting and I think that

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:22.900
the flexibility in the deal-making

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:24.180
that's happening behind the scenes

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:25.900
is enabling things like this to occur.

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:29.684
The friction's starting to
go away, which is exciting.

00:16:29.684 --> 00:16:31.060
You asked me earlier,

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:32.977
what's the thing I was
most surprised by to learn?

00:16:32.977 --> 00:16:33.810
- [Gary] Yep.

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:36.580
- It's about, so I had
this realization this year.

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:38.920
We started sitting down
with a lot of artists

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:40.790
who started on Stem

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:43.420
and were getting pursued by major labels.

00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:44.570
And the major labels were putting these

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:45.830
insane deals in front of them,

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:48.730
with huge cash up-front advances,

00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:50.930
and the way that recruitment works

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:53.110
in the business is fucking crazy.

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:54.920
But we can talk about that separately.

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:56.600
And so when I would look at these deals,

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:57.433
I would say to the artist,

00:16:57.433 --> 00:16:59.470
"Do you know what you're giving up?"

00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:01.387
And they'd say, "Well,
my manager's helping me

00:17:01.387 --> 00:17:02.557
"navigate the deal terms

00:17:02.557 --> 00:17:05.000
"with my lawyer or business manager."

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:06.833
And I'd say, "Okay, has anyone
built you a financial model?

00:17:06.833 --> 00:17:08.537
"You know how much money
you're making right now,

00:17:08.537 --> 00:17:10.747
"because you're self-distributing.

00:17:10.747 --> 00:17:13.587
"How much money are you
gonna give up over time

00:17:13.587 --> 00:17:14.420
"to pay this back?

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:16.397
"And by the way, are you
ever gonna pay it back?

00:17:16.397 --> 00:17:19.230
"Will you see another dollar
after this big advance?"

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:21.620
And what blew me away
is that when an artist

00:17:21.620 --> 00:17:23.820
is negotiating terms
between two different offers

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:25.490
or looking at any offer,

00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:27.995
no one writes them a financial model.

00:17:27.995 --> 00:17:30.040
Like, no one does.

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:31.480
Whether it's an up-and-coming artist

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:33.500
or someone who's huge, that's established,

00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:35.080
that's renegotiating their deals,

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:36.770
could you imagine taking an investment

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:38.800
for your business without
seeing a proforma?

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:39.633
- [Gary] Of course not.

00:17:39.633 --> 00:17:41.680
- Like, how do you know
what you're gonna do

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:43.070
without knowing how much you're giving up?

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:44.177
- So when you're showing them that like,

00:17:44.177 --> 00:17:45.327
"Cool, you're getting this check,

00:17:45.327 --> 00:17:47.217
"but FYI do you realize that you would've

00:17:47.217 --> 00:17:49.367
"actually made this money in 27 months

00:17:49.367 --> 00:17:50.687
"and then own your own shit

00:17:50.687 --> 00:17:53.510
"and then keep making
money versus never again?"

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:55.170
It becomes a very different conversation.

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:56.420
- Yeah, it completely does.

00:17:56.420 --> 00:17:58.049
And I think people don't understand

00:17:58.049 --> 00:18:00.050
what these deal terms mean, right?

00:18:00.050 --> 00:18:01.250
The most artist-friendly deal

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:03.270
is considered a 50-50 net profit.

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:04.980
It's like a JV, right?

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:06.020
Like, I'm putting up the money,

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:06.980
you and I are gonna be partners,

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:08.600
we're gonna share the profits, right?

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:09.740
Sounds amazing.

00:18:09.740 --> 00:18:11.190
- Yeah, except back in the day,

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:13.670
there was actually risk for these labels

00:18:13.670 --> 00:18:15.650
because you really didn't know.

00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:17.820
Now, they're not signing people

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:19.840
until the market's been
established for them anyway.

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:22.496
It's super-easy for them to
hedge against the downside.

00:18:22.496 --> 00:18:23.730
- Completely.

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:24.930
And they're underwriting their risk

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:28.448
the same way for someone who's
proven a bit of traction--

00:18:28.448 --> 00:18:30.206
- 'Course.
- To someone that's completely

00:18:30.206 --> 00:18:32.554
- Maybe, amend the stal, these
are obvious before they sign.

00:18:32.554 --> 00:18:34.170
- Right.

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.158
And they have historical
revenue that you can look at

00:18:36.158 --> 00:18:37.840
to underwrite and they just don't.

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.310
It's a bidding war.
- [Gary] Yep.

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:43.000
- And so we announced earlier this year

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:44.400
a program called the Stem Check,

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:46.190
just because I was so frustrated

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:48.890
that I saw people taking deals
that they didn't understand.

00:18:48.890 --> 00:18:51.270
And the purpose of the Stem
Check is it's open office hours,

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:52.700
come sit with my finance team.

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:56.150
I hired amazing people
from companies like SpaceX

00:18:57.448 --> 00:18:59.410
and Yahoo Finance and other firms

00:18:59.410 --> 00:19:01.580
that are really well
established with strong teams.

00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:03.000
Come bring your deal terms.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:04.320
They'll look at them.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:06.350
Or even just tell them
hypothetical deal terms,

00:19:06.350 --> 00:19:07.183
we'll look at your revenue,

00:19:07.183 --> 00:19:08.230
we'll build you a model so you can

00:19:08.230 --> 00:19:11.850
at least see what it means
to give up 50%, 30%, 70%.

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:14.638
And how does it look like
when you're recouping against

00:19:14.638 --> 00:19:16.510
the gross not your share.

00:19:16.510 --> 00:19:17.583
- Are the labels starting to understand

00:19:17.583 --> 00:19:20.180
that they need to provide
more value than distribution

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:21.360
or still not yet?

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:22.193
- Oh yeah.

00:19:22.193 --> 00:19:25.330
I think they're realizing that, you know,

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:27.520
they need to bring
these services in-house.

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:31.720
I think one of the biggest
changes was Napster.

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:34.060
And people talk about it
because it created new platforms

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:37.510
but what people forget
is that when Napster

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:39.090
blew up the music business,

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:41.870
people dropped all of
their shared services.

00:19:41.870 --> 00:19:44.010
So the freelance people
who did radio and marketing

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:45.710
that were once employed
by the major labels

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:47.200
are no longer employed.

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:48.640
- You know what's funny about Napster?

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:50.426
I was more of a ShareBear user.

00:19:50.426 --> 00:19:51.290
(Phil laughing)

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:53.070
Do you remember ShareBear, Phil?

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:56.180
I still, like ShareBear was my Napster.

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:58.810
That was a shout-out to
all the '90s tech kids.

00:19:58.810 --> 00:19:59.643
Keep going.

00:19:59.643 --> 00:20:02.050
- Yes, so all these radio promo people

00:20:02.050 --> 00:20:03.380
that you think are only hireable

00:20:03.380 --> 00:20:06.440
because they're employees
of the record label

00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:07.470
are actually freelancers.

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:09.820
So if you have money,
you can hire them, too.

00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:12.890
So at this point, I think
major labels for the most part

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:15.682
are big banks.
- Of course they are.

00:20:15.682 --> 00:20:16.880
- They're really expensive, right?

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:17.713
- [Gary] Of course they are.

00:20:17.713 --> 00:20:18.710
- They do distribution
but you don't need a label

00:20:18.710 --> 00:20:20.013
for distribution any more.

00:20:20.013 --> 00:20:21.120
- No, there's something
called "the internet."

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:21.953
- Yeah.

00:20:21.953 --> 00:20:23.980
But what you do still need and
I think this is really rare,

00:20:23.980 --> 00:20:27.210
is an A&amp;R, like that person who
has a vision for you, right?

00:20:27.210 --> 00:20:29.840
Like bringing it back to
the Jimmy Iovine days.

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:31.960
- So more Vince McMahon, right?

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:32.793
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:20:32.793 --> 00:20:34.750
- Someone who sees you
and knows how to package

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:37.830
and merchandise you in
an authentic way to you,

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:40.130
instead of making you
do something you're not.

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:41.220
And take it from there.

00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:44.440
- Yeah, because that's a
real partnership, right?

00:20:44.440 --> 00:20:45.273
- [Gary] Right.

00:20:45.273 --> 00:20:46.106
- And that's real talent development.

00:20:46.106 --> 00:20:46.950
But they can't do that
when they're signing

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:48.350
thousands of artists a year.

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:54.087
So, knowing that, when
we look at our business--

00:20:54.087 --> 00:20:56.360
- So, if you're talking to
your hypothetical sister,

00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:59.069
who happens to be the next Billie Eilish.

00:20:59.069 --> 00:20:59.902
- [Milana] Sure.

00:20:59.902 --> 00:21:01.610
- And she's coming up,
what do you say to her?

00:21:01.610 --> 00:21:02.900
How do you see it?

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:05.150
If she's like, "Hey,
Milana, what should I do?"

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:06.690
Give me the blueprint.

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:08.460
What is your personal opinion.
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:09.800
- Obviously you've built a company around

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:11.560
parts of it but there's
a little bit more to that

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:13.846
which is why I'm asking
what do you tell her?

00:21:13.846 --> 00:21:15.407
- Depends on what you
think you're capable of

00:21:15.407 --> 00:21:16.450
and what you need, right?

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:17.932
What are the jobs that you need done?

00:21:17.932 --> 00:21:19.930
- Well, fair enough.

00:21:19.930 --> 00:21:21.400
But I think what's most interesting

00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:24.647
is that everybody at that
stage reverse-engineers from

00:21:24.647 --> 00:21:26.760
"I wanna be the biggest
star in the world."

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:28.040
- Yeah, but you can get to being

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:28.873
the biggest star in the world

00:21:28.873 --> 00:21:30.100
if you have the right
team around you, right?

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:31.270
But there's some people who don't know

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:32.770
how to put that team together.
- [Gary] Most.

00:21:32.770 --> 00:21:34.190
- Who don't want to manage that team.

00:21:34.190 --> 00:21:35.023
- [Gary] Most.

00:21:35.023 --> 00:21:35.856
- And so that's okay,

00:21:35.856 --> 00:21:37.390
go to a place that'll do it for you.

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:40.130
But if you're someone
like a Chance the Rapper

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:42.650
or a Frank Ocean or
someone who has the ability

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:43.960
to have a team around them,

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:47.290
and all you need is just tools and money,

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:50.950
there's great options for you
to go get that on your own.

00:21:50.950 --> 00:21:52.140
- And how do you think that's playing out

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:55.410
for non-musicians, aka creators on TikTok

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:57.579
and YouTube and other
platforms that will emerge?

00:21:57.579 --> 00:21:59.620
- There's a difference, right?

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:02.550
Like, we saw MCNs which
were the equivalent

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:04.340
of record labels and they all fell apart.

00:22:04.340 --> 00:22:05.700
- Well, they were all over-leveraged

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:07.398
and got over-valued.
- [Milana] Right.

00:22:07.398 --> 00:22:09.150
But they weren't really sustainable.

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:11.110
- No, but I think the question becomes,

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:12.900
it's like direct-to-consumer
brands, right?

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:13.733
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:22:13.733 --> 00:22:16.840
- Most fail, most things
fail when they're over-valued

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:18.880
so extreme and people are
taking cash off the table.

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:20.290
That's just financial arbitrage.

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:21.123
- [Milana] Sure.

00:22:21.123 --> 00:22:21.956
- Right?

00:22:21.956 --> 00:22:22.789
And then you have founders,

00:22:22.789 --> 00:22:24.730
and a lot of those MCNs
had that scenario where,

00:22:24.730 --> 00:22:26.460
once you make that big payday,

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:28.580
the energy of building it
goes to a different place

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:29.720
because now you wanna buy a boat

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:31.380
or a nice house in the hills

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:34.468
instead of actually building
something meaningful forever.

00:22:34.468 --> 00:22:38.050
To your point, the theory of it, MCN,

00:22:38.050 --> 00:22:40.984
or theory of a manager
or an agent or a platform

00:22:40.984 --> 00:22:43.910
can play out if it
continues to bring value

00:22:43.910 --> 00:22:44.940
to the end user.

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:47.690
- It can, you're right,
but, I've even seen it.

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:49.010
- Do you see a lot of these people

00:22:49.010 --> 00:22:51.310
just becoming insular
businesses themselves?

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:55.110
Aka, two to three
meaningful tools around them

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:57.370
and then six or seven
meaningful humans around them

00:22:57.370 --> 00:22:58.520
and then that's the game?

00:22:58.520 --> 00:22:59.890
- Yeah, I mean, that's where you see

00:22:59.890 --> 00:23:01.634
a lot of the big management companies were

00:23:01.634 --> 00:23:04.640
built off the back of a big artist.

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:07.450
Red Light was built off the
back of Dave Mathews Band.

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:09.810
Rock Nation was built
on the back of Jay-Z.

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:13.270
So you start building a team
around an artist in-house,

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:14.894
and then you start signing more artists

00:23:14.894 --> 00:23:17.110
because you now have a team
- [Gary] The leverage.

00:23:17.110 --> 00:23:17.943
- That can scale.

00:23:17.943 --> 00:23:18.776
- [Gary] Yeah.

00:23:18.776 --> 00:23:20.370
- So, absolutely.

00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:22.830
And I think that the new,

00:23:22.830 --> 00:23:24.930
all the services that a
record label used to provide,

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:26.580
a lot of that work is happening

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:28.009
inside of a management company now.

00:23:28.009 --> 00:23:28.842
- I think that's right.

00:23:28.842 --> 00:23:31.450
So what didn't we, now
as we're wrapping up,

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:32.283
like what--
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:23:32.283 --> 00:23:33.370
- Like what else do you wanna talk about?

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:35.250
- So the thing we're gonna announce soon

00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:37.280
and that I'm excited about is that

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:39.904
we just partnered with
a firm called CoVenture.

00:23:39.904 --> 00:23:40.900
- [Gary] CoVenture.

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:44.044
- And we raised $100 million
to invest in artists.

00:23:44.044 --> 00:23:45.370
- Explain.

00:23:45.370 --> 00:23:48.380
- So, I think that we talked about

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:52.270
how predatory the nature
of many advances are.

00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:54.920
And we wanna be able to
not only educate artists

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:56.530
about what they're giving up

00:23:56.530 --> 00:23:58.310
but also offer an alternative.

00:23:58.310 --> 00:24:01.530
- Right, so, because the deals are bad,

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:04.455
you can go in between "bad" and "zero"

00:24:04.455 --> 00:24:07.700
and make them what you deem as the best

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:09.650
alternative to the reality
of the marketplace.

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:11.715
- Yeah, more fairly-priced capital.

00:24:11.715 --> 00:24:12.940
And what I think is--

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:14.260
- The end.
- Well, yeah.

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:15.093
- [Gary] It makes sense.

00:24:15.093 --> 00:24:17.750
- But it's meant to be more
than just fairly priced.

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:18.880
How do you know it's fair, right?

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:21.250
Well, you have to know how
much are you taking out?

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:22.930
How much do you have to pay back?

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:24.760
And how long is it gonna take you?

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:27.740
- In essence, historically, like the best

00:24:27.740 --> 00:24:31.020
moneylenders of all time
built the biggest companies

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:33.030
in the world which is how
do you get more aligned

00:24:33.030 --> 00:24:34.351
with the person that's taking the money?

00:24:34.351 --> 00:24:35.210
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:38.210
- Why did banks do a better
job than loan sharks?

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:41.060
It was better for the
person taking the money.

00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:43.050
- And not only can you get the money,

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:45.040
but you can also get
the resources with us.

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:46.620
- Understood, smart money.

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:47.530
- Smart money.

00:24:47.530 --> 00:24:49.060
Money that scales.

00:24:49.060 --> 00:24:50.160
And we're calling it "Scale."

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:52.720
- And so, what, this
is your play into like,

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:54.870
is this a payback model where
you're in the financial,

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:56.360
or are you trying to actually have

00:24:56.360 --> 00:24:58.970
a 3% of the back-end of Eminem?

00:24:58.970 --> 00:25:00.422
- No, I don't want any back-end.

00:25:00.422 --> 00:25:03.980
If you're making 10 grand a
month off your music on Stem,

00:25:03.980 --> 00:25:05.620
- [Gary] Yep, yep.
- Your share, by the way.

00:25:05.620 --> 00:25:09.130
You can take around $100,000 let's say,

00:25:09.130 --> 00:25:10.960
and you can decide do you wanna pay it off

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:12.890
with 100% of your revenue every month?

00:25:12.890 --> 00:25:15.930
Or do you need 5K in your
pocket to pay your rent

00:25:15.930 --> 00:25:16.763
and other expenses.

00:25:16.763 --> 00:25:18.150
- And you're making a
fee against the money.

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:20.630
- Well, you're contributing
whatever you want.

00:25:20.630 --> 00:25:22.350
Depending on how much you wanna contribute

00:25:22.350 --> 00:25:23.950
will determine how
quickly you pay it down.

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:25.670
So the faster you pay it
down, the smaller the fee.

00:25:25.670 --> 00:25:26.503
- [Gary] I understand.

00:25:26.503 --> 00:25:28.411
- And so you as the artist
are the person taking it out--

00:25:28.411 --> 00:25:29.573
- And for you from a business standpoint,

00:25:29.573 --> 00:25:31.490
because you have the math of
what they're doing on Stem,

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:33.810
you guys have a clean dataset which allows

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:36.180
you to give away money at low risk

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:38.080
and the lower the risk is,

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:39.990
the lower the percentage you charge them

00:25:39.990 --> 00:25:41.510
based on how fast they pay back.

00:25:41.510 --> 00:25:43.200
And so you're just, it makes sense.

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:44.610
- And we're in the collection flow, right?

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:45.443
The money comes through us--

00:25:45.443 --> 00:25:46.920
- You're in the credit card business.

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:48.140
- I don't know if it's a
credit card business but--

00:25:48.140 --> 00:25:50.450
- Well, you know, with a lot of data,

00:25:50.450 --> 00:25:52.950
you'll have a really
good sense of how risky

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:55.050
the advances you're giving are

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:57.700
and you make small
percentages in the scheme

00:25:57.700 --> 00:25:58.880
- [Milana] Yeah.
- Versus the big percentages

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:01.466
that the labels are doing.
- Yeah, and I actually believe

00:26:01.466 --> 00:26:02.479
- [Gary] Or are there alternate things?

00:26:02.479 --> 00:26:03.620
- That an artist doesn't have to take

00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:04.640
that big check up front,

00:26:04.640 --> 00:26:05.610
they should be taking,

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:07.190
and we're incentivizing
them with the model

00:26:07.190 --> 00:26:08.463
to take the money they need right now,

00:26:08.463 --> 00:26:11.310
because it's a line of credit,
they can take more over time.

00:26:11.310 --> 00:26:13.190
- And the labels and others
will still be in business

00:26:13.190 --> 00:26:14.380
because some people are impatient

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:16.340
and the thought of a $2 million check

00:26:16.340 --> 00:26:18.010
still excites them even
though it's a bad deal.

00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:19.440
- And the alignment with a big brand,

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:20.520
like a Universal,
- [Gary] Right.

00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:21.710
- Or an Interscope or an
Atlantic is still attractive.

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:23.230
- Or telling your homies back at home,

00:26:23.230 --> 00:26:24.830
like, "I signed this deal."
- [Milana] Yeah.

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:28.600
- So it's brand and maybe
the glitz of a bigger deal

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:30.270
which is less practical than something

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:32.183
that's a little bit more
financially responsible.

00:26:32.183 --> 00:26:33.542
- Yeah, and listen,
- [Gary] Makes sense.

00:26:33.542 --> 00:26:34.397
- There's always gonna be people

00:26:34.397 --> 00:26:35.230
who make those decisions, right?

00:26:35.230 --> 00:26:36.063
- Yeah.

00:26:36.063 --> 00:26:37.280
There's somebody right now
who just borrowed money

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:39.470
paying 40% interest to
some very shady person

00:26:39.470 --> 00:26:40.330
that's gonna break their neck

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:42.000
if they don't pay it back in 100 days.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:43.440
That's real life, that happens.

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:45.374
- [Milana] Totally does,
but at least we wanna have--

00:26:45.374 --> 00:26:46.320
- Phil's done that a couple of times.

00:26:46.320 --> 00:26:47.153
- [Milana] Oh, God, Phil,
please don't tell me.

00:26:47.153 --> 00:26:47.986
- But he's still here.

00:26:47.986 --> 00:26:49.176
He always pulls it out in the end.

00:26:49.176 --> 00:26:50.550
(laughing)

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:53.000
- But I think that for a
person to start thinking about

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:55.010
their business in terms of
how much money am I taking

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:56.480
and what does it mean
that I have to pay back?

00:26:56.480 --> 00:26:57.750
Is a concept that people in music

00:26:57.750 --> 00:26:59.120
just haven't thought about it that way.

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:00.089
- [Gary] I get it.

00:27:00.089 --> 00:27:01.040
- They think about, hey, there's no fee,

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:02.410
